 "preamble": "Each across clue is a passage from which the printer has omitted a number of consecutive letters. These form the word which answers the clue. On closing the gap, he may have altered punctuation and spacing, but he has not disturbed the order of the remaining letters. E.g. ‘To please the mobile toffs: tea-urn game (7)’ leads to MINARET (‘To please the mob I let off steam in a return game’). In each down clue, the printer has substituted a single false letter for one other, always within the definition part of the clue. E.g. ‘Hardy’s strange tale (4)’ leads to LATE (‘Tardy’s strange tale’). In memory of Afrit and Ximenes, inventors of the across and the down clue-types respectively.",
